# CASP15 T1118 — Bacterial Iron Piracy (8B43)
---

Bacteria are constantly at war — with us,
and with each other — over one critical resource: iron.

This is FoxA, a TonB-dependent transporter from
Pseudomonas aeruginosa.

Published in PNAS in 2023 by Chan and colleagues.

> close
> load 8b43
---

FoxA sits in the outer membrane of Pseudomonas
and acts like a gateway for siderophores —
small molecules that bacteria secrete
to steal iron from their environment.

Iron is so scarce and so essential that
bacteria have evolved elaborate piracy
systems to capture it.

> spin 0.15
---

Here's where siderophores and antibiotics bind.

The remarkable thing about FoxA is that
some antibiotics hijack this transporter —
they disguise themselves as siderophores
to sneak into the bacterial cell.

It's a Trojan horse strategy for killing bacteria.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Pseudomonas aeruginosa is one of the most
dangerous hospital-acquired infections,
and it's increasingly antibiotic resistant.

Understanding transporters like FoxA could lead
to new antibiotics that exploit the bacteria's
own iron-stealing machinery.

> reset
> spin 0.1
